.SH "OPTION VALUES"
These pages write an option's value as a separate word: \fBubertooth\-util \-c 2440\fR, \fBubertooth\-btle \-a 8e89bed6\fR, \fBubertooth\-rx \-l 446688 \-u 22\fR\. This follows a standard, the POSIX Utility Syntax Guidelines (guideline 6: an option and its value are separate arguments)\. The value placeholders are written the same way (\fB\-c <2402\-2480>\fR)\. The older attached form is still accepted for backward compatibility with existing scripts, so \fB\-c 2440\fR and \fB\-c2440\fR are the same\. Where the value is optional, the next word is taken as the value only when it is a valid value, so an option never swallows the option after it: \fB\-c \-b\fR reads the channel and then the board ID, while \fB\-z \-90\fR sets the squelch to \-90 dBm\. A long option takes \fB\-\-name value\fR or \fB\-\-name=value\fR, never attached without the \fB=\fR (no long option takes a value today; this is for consistency with \fBgetopt_long\fR)\. A lone \fB\-\fR as the value of a file option means standard input or standard output, so \fBubertooth\-btle \-q \-\fR writes the capture to stdout\.
.SH "SELECTING A DEVICE"
With a single Ubertooth attached, the tools find it automatically\. With more than one attached, every tool takes \fB\-U <index|serial>\fR to choose one: an index is a position in the list of attached Ubertooths (\fB\-U 0\fR is the first), and a serial\-number string selects that board whatever the enumeration order\. An index that is not attached is refused (\fBerror: no Ubertooth at index 2 (2 attached)\fR) and the tool exits non\-zero\. Indexes are positions in the list of boards attached when the command runs, so they shift when a board is added or removed; to keep targeting the same board, select it by serial\.
.P
A serial may be shortened to its first few characters, in either case, so \fB\-U 1cc0\fR and \fB\-U 1CC0\fR select the same board\. If those characters start more than one attached board's serial, the tool lists the matching serials and selects neither\. An all\-digits string is read as an index, so include a non\-digit (\fB\-U 05f\fR rather than \fB\-U 05\fR)\. See ubertooth\-util(1), SELECTING A DEVICE, for the full description and for listing the attached devices\.
.SH "OUTPUT AND DIAGNOSTICS"
This is the split the tools are being brought to, tool by tool\. Each tool's own page says what it does today, under DIAGNOSTICS and EXIT STATUS; where a page has neither section yet, that tool has not been converted\.
.P
Standard output carries only what was asked for: the report a query returns, the packets or samples a capture decodes, the capture data itself when the file argument is \fB\-\fR, and the usage text for \fB\-h\fR\. Everything else goes to standard error, so a script reading a tool's standard output is not fed a diagnostic or a progress line\.
.P
Errors are one line starting \fBerror:\fR; warnings start \fBwarning:\fR and notes start \fBnote:\fR\. Progress and status ("Resetting \|\.\|\.\|\.", "Ubertooth stopped", DFU progress) carry no prefix\. The usage text goes to standard output for \fB\-h\fR and to standard error, after the \fBerror:\fR line, when the command line was wrong or had nothing to do\.
.P
Exit status is 0 only when the command did what was asked, including \fB\-h\fR, and 1 otherwise: a usage error, a command line with nothing to do, a device that is missing or failed, a partial failure\. ubertooth\-afh(1) also uses 2, for a detection phase that received no packet\.
.P
libubertooth follows the split already: it returns what it reads to the tool that called it and reports its own problems on standard error, so a library message does not land in the middle of a capture\. The clock trim and AFH channel notes raised while following a piconet are \fBnote:\fR lines on standard error\. What the library still writes to standard output is the calling tool's result \(em the decoded packet reports, the AFH map, the version line \(em which is the right stream for them, whichever layer prints them\. Its device\-selection messages are on standard error but do not all carry a prefix yet\.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
\fBUBERTOOTH_TEST_NO_DEVICES=1\fR makes every tool behave as if no Ubertooth were attached: nothing is listed or opened on the USB bus, and the tool reports that it found no device\. It exists for the project's own test suite, which runs the tools while boards may be in use elsewhere, and it is the only setting that changes device selection\. Only the exact value \fB1\fR turns it on; unset, empty or any other value leaves device selection alone\. A tool that finds no board because of it says so: \fBnote: UBERTOOTH_TEST_NO_DEVICES is set, so no board is selected\fR\.
